WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
in this lesson we're going to talk about  an important concept and that's knowing  how to graph parabolas so let's get  right into this lesson there's three  different forms okay of  the equation for a parabola the first  one is what they call the general form  or the standard form sometimes it's this  y equals ax squared plus bx plus c  okay and the way you would find the the  vertex of the parabola the parabola you  know bends like so this turning point is  you're going to use the formula  x equals negative b  over 2a  so here's your b value here's your a  value and that will allow you to find  the x coordinate of the vertex and then  by putting that back in you can find the  y coordinate  so let's go ahead and do this one first  and then we'll talk about the two other  forms so in this case you can see that  the opposite of b the opposite of  negative six is going to be positive six  over two times a which is three so that  comes out to six over six which is one  now if we put one back into our equation  in place of x we get let's see one  squared is one times three is three  six times one is six  plus one we get negative three plus one  which is negative two so our vertex is  going to be at one negative two so it's  going to be right there  now from here you can use a couple  different techniques one technique is  you could just  you know plot some values and usually  what i would do is pick some values on  either side of the  vertex because that vertex is going to  be  where the axis of symmetry goes through  meaning it's the same on both sides so  for example if i put zero in for x i can  see that the y intercept is one so i  know it's crossing right here and if i  reflect that over the line of symmetry i  have another point on the graph so  that's a quick way to  to get a sketch of your parabola the  other way to do it is you can focus in  on the parent function which is the y  equals ax squared part of the equation  and then what you would do is you would  make a table like an xy table and you  would just pick some values so for  example 1 squared is 1 times 3 is 3  2 squared is 4 times 3 is 12 and then  what you would do is you would plot  these points  from the vertex as if that was the  origin or the starting point so you  would go right one up 3 left one up 3  right 2 up 12 and left 2 up 12  because it's the same on both sides so  that's the way you would work with  a parabola or quadratic when it's in  this general form or standard form  however you want to say it and that  would be to memorize this formula x  equals negative b over 2a  now the second form here is what they  call the vertex form okay and then you  can see that h and k okay those are  going to be the coordinates of the  vertex so in this example you can see  that  the vertex is going to be at positive 1  positive 3. now what you want to  remember is that the one this group with  the x actually has the opposite effect  on the graph this is actually going to  shift everything positive one or right  one and this one has the same effect as  the sign so positive would go up  negative it would go down but just  remember the one group of the x has the  opposite effect so here our vertex is  going to be at 1  3 so right there there's our axis of  symmetry that goes right through that  okay that vertex and then what you can  do is if you look at this a value here  negative 2 that's going to be the same  as the a value over here okay and what  you can see is that the parent function  or the basic shape of the graph is going  to be y equals negative 2x squared and  we can make a table of values for  example one two  okay like that one squared is one times  negative two is negative two  two squared is four times negative two  is negative eight so what i would do is  i would plot those points from this  vertex right here i'm gonna go right one  down two left one down two then same  thing here i can go right two down eight  and left two down eight but you can see  that this graph is going to look  like this now again once you find that  vertex and you see that a value is  negative you know it's going to be  opening down if a was positive it would  open up like this you could just you  know pick some points just maybe like  zero is always an easy point to pick  once you find that point you just  reflect it over the line of symmetry and  you've got your graph  so that's the vertex form and then this  third form here is what they call the  intercept form  and you can see that it's basically like  a factored form you can see it's you  know factored into two binomials and  what you do here is you set each group  each factor equal to zero so you can see  that x equals one and x x equals three  so by setting these to zero and solving  that allows you to find the the  x-intercept so we know it's crossing  here at one and here at three  and then the way parabolas are  is that they're going to be symmetric so  you can find the midpoint between these  two points and that's going to be your  axis of symmetry or your line of  symmetry and then you can see that line  of symmetry is going right through x  equals 2  so by taking 2 and putting it back in to  our equation we can find the y  coordinate of the vertex so let's go  ahead and do that so 2 minus 1 is going  to be 1. 2 minus 3 is negative 1. 3  times 1 is 3 times negative 1 is  negative 3 so that means i'm going to be  down here at 2 negative 3. that's the  vertex and if you go ahead and graph  through those three points you get a  pretty good idea of what the graph looks  like so again  intercept form is like factored form you  set the factors to zero to find the x  intercepts you find the halfway point  between those two x intercepts that's  your line of symmetry once you know that  x value put it back in to find the y  value of the vertex then you've got  three points and you can get a good  graph so definitely an important concept  to understand knowing how to graph  parabolas i'll see you in the next  lesson

